---
import DocumentLayout from '../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';
import CrossLinks from '../components/CrossLinks.astro';
import { goals, values, requirements, checked } from '../data/rubric.mjs';
import Needs from '../components/Needs.astro';
import Draft from '../components/Draft.astro';
import { audit } from '../data/palette.mjs';
import { REPO, REPO_LABEL } from '../data/site.mjs';

// Computed at build time from the same module the pre-build check runs against.
// A hand-typed table of passing numbers would be a claim; this is the check.
const rows = audit();
const passing = rows.filter((r) => r.pass).length;
---

<DocumentLayout
  title="How this site is built"
  metaTitle="Colophon | Tyler Young"
  description="What this portfolio is built with, and the checks that run against it on every build."
  eyebrow="Colophon"
  meta={[
    ['Stack', 'Astro · React islands · Tailwind · static output'],
    ['Client JS', 'None on content pages'],
    ['Checks', 'Contrast audit · open-items audit · both on every build'],
    ['Source', `<a href="${REPO}">${REPO_LABEL}</a>`],
  ]}
>
  <p class="opening">
    <Draft>
      Sixteen static pages. No client JavaScript on any of them, fonts served from
      the same origin, and two checks that run on every build and can fail it.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>The standard it is held to</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Written before the redesign and used to settle arguments during it. Goals
      say what success looks like. Values say what will not be traded to get
      there. Requirements are the testable form of both — and each one records
      whether a machine checks it or a person does, because a requirement with no
      check is an intention.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <dl class="rubric">
    {
      goals.map((g) => (
        <div class="rubric-row">
          <dt>{g.goal}</dt>
          <dd>{g.because}</dd>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </dl>

  <p class="rubric-label">Values</p>
  <dl class="rubric">
    {
      values.map((v) => (
        <div class="rubric-row">
          <dt>{v.value}</dt>
          <dd>{v.means}</dd>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </dl>

  <p class="rubric-label">
    Requirements — {checked} of {requirements.length} checked by a script on every build
  </p>
  <ul class="reqs">
    {
      requirements.map((r) => (
        <li class:list={['req', { auto: r.check }]}>
          <span class="req-mark" aria-hidden="true" />
          <span class="req-text">
            {r.req}
            <span class="req-detail">
              {r.check ? `npm run ${r.check} — ${r.detail}` : `by eye — ${r.detail}`}
            </span>
          </span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <h2>Reading the old site first</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      I started by pulling every word off the live site — nine essays, the TL;DR,
      three project cards, three references — and reading it as a stranger would.
      Two things came out of that. The argument was leadership: frameworks,
      scoring models, a seat at the planning table. And the evidence was decks.
      Nothing on it could be opened.
    </Draft>
  </p>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Reading it that closely also turned up defects I had shipped without
      noticing. The same alt text on three different people's headshots. Nine more
      images with none at all.
      <code>&lt;meta name="description" content="Made with Framer"&gt;</code> on
      every essay page. Body text at 2.6:1 against its background. Three essays
      sharing whole paragraphs word for word.
    </Draft>
  </p>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      None of that is unusual for a site built in a visual editor. All of it is
      what an engineering team checks in the first thirty seconds.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>Deciding what had to change</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      The reader I care about gives a portfolio ninety seconds, on bad wifi,
      between two other tabs. That is the whole brief. It rules out a slow hero, a
      carousel, a case study that explains its methodology before showing
      anything.
    </Draft>
  </p>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      So the order changed. Three stories, each opening on something running, at
      the top of the page. The leadership essays moved behind them — not because
      they matter less, but because a claim about how I lead is worth more after
      you have seen what I ship than before. The hero came out entirely: a name
      and a bio line were pushing the only evidence on the page below the fold.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>Building it</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      The agent wrote the code. I decided what it was allowed to write, read
      everything that came back, and rejected the parts that cost more than they
      returned. The style was settled the same way anything gets settled — in
      passes. Warm paper became white. A serif body became sans, and the serif
      came out of the build entirely once nothing referenced it. The header went
      through two colours before landing.
    </Draft>
  </p>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Working in code is what made that cheap. Changing every page's background is
      one token. Checking that the change did not break contrast anywhere is one
      command. In a visual editor both are an afternoon, which is why they usually
      do not happen.
    </Draft>
  </p>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      It also caught its own mistakes. The first pass at porting the essays
      dropped all fifteen diagrams — the scoring rubrics, the heat map, the
      two-by-twos — and left the arguments without their evidence. Recovering them
      was possible because the source was a file I could diff, not a canvas I had
      to remember.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>What runs on every build</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Two checks, both written for this site, both in the repo. Neither is an
      off-the-shelf linter. They test decisions specific to this project.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h3>Contrast audit</h3>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Every colour pair in the design system, checked at the smallest size it is
      used at. It failed twice while I was building this. Two tokens got darker
      until it passed.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <caption class="label">
        Computed at build time from <code>src/data/palette.mjs</code> — {passing} of {rows.length} pairs passing
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Pair</th>
          <th scope="col">Ratio</th>
          <th scope="col">Needs</th>
          <th scope="col">Used for</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          rows.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td>
                <span class="swatch" style={`--fg:var(--color-${r.fg});--bg:var(--color-${r.bg})`}>
                  Aa
                </span>
                {r.fg} on {r.bg}
              </td>
              <td class="num">{r.ratio.toFixed(2)}</td>
              <td class="num dim">{r.need.toFixed(2)}</td>
              <td class="use">{r.use}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>Open-items audit</h3>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Scans the built HTML for unfinished slots and unapproved copy, and reports
      where each one is. The list comes from the build, not from a checklist I keep
      by hand, so it cannot go stale. With <code>--strict</code> it fails the
      build.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>What this site does</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No client JavaScript on content pages — view source and count the script tags</li>
    <li>One <code>h1</code> per page, headings in order, no skipped levels</li>
    <li>Real alt text on every image, or no image yet</li>
    <li>Visible keyboard focus, reduced motion respected, skip link</li>
    <li>Self-hosted fonts, latin subsets only, no third-party requests</li>
    <li>Old essay URLs preserved exactly, so existing links survive</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The same loop produced this page.
  </p>

  <CrossLinks
    slot="after"
    label="Related"
    title="The practice behind it"
    links={[
      { href: '/how-i-design-with-ai', label: 'I design with AI' },
      { href: '/atheer-design-pipeline', label: 'The design-to-code pipeline' },
    ]}
  />
</DocumentLayout>

<style>
  .opening {
    font-size: 24px !important;
    line-height: 1.5 !important;
    margin-bottom: 34px !important;
  }

  .table-wrap {
    margin: 0 0 30px;
    overflow-x: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    background: var(--color-paper-raised);
  }

  table {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 540px;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.03em;
  }

  caption {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 12px 14px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    color: var(--color-ink-3);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  th {
    text-align: left;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    font-size: 10px;
    color: var(--color-ink-3);
    padding: 10px 14px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
  }

  td {
    padding: 8px 14px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    color: var(--color-ink-2);
    white-space: nowrap;
    vertical-align: middle;
  }

  tr:last-child td {
    border-bottom: 0;
  }

  .num {
    color: var(--color-ink);
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .dim {
    color: var(--color-ink-3);
  }

  .use {
    white-space: normal;
    color: var(--color-ink-3);
  }

  .swatch {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    width: 26px;
    height: 18px;
    margin-right: 10px;
    vertical-align: -4px;
    background: var(--bg);
    color: var(--fg);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    font-size: 10px;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.82em;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    background: var(--color-paper-sunk);
    padding: 1px 5px;
  }
</style>
